WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.050 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.470 align:middle line:84%
The following content is
provided under a Creative

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:03.880 align:middle line:90%
Commons license.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:06.920 align:middle line:84%
Your support will help MIT
OpenCourseWare continue to

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:10.570 align:middle line:84%
offer high quality educational
resources for free.

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:13.470 align:middle line:84%
To make a donation or view
additional materials from

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:19.290 align:middle line:84%
hundreds of MIT courses, visit
MIT OpenCourseWare at

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:20.720 align:middle line:90%
ocw.mit.edu.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.919 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: One example will be
what happens when the battery

00:00:23.919 --> 00:00:26.100 align:middle line:84%
runs up on the computer
and what bubbles

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.500 align:middle line:90%
up when it's running.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.520 align:middle line:84%
And there's certain computers
that might [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.050 align:middle line:90%
hard drives.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:34.090 align:middle line:84%
Some computers just keep it in
RAM when you restart it.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.670 align:middle line:90%
There's no big difference.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:40.188 align:middle line:84%
What happens when an IM window
pops up in the middle of the

00:00:40.188 --> 00:00:43.667 align:middle line:84%
game and leaves the
control screen?

00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:46.152 align:middle line:84%
And all the keys start
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:00:46.152 --> 00:00:48.140 align:middle line:90%
IM window.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:50.836 align:middle line:84%
Ideally, they give you pop
ups that doesn't work.

00:00:50.836 --> 00:00:54.730 align:middle line:84%
But catching those is one of our
favorite things that we do

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:59.032 align:middle line:84%
when we're making digital games
here in the lab is like

00:00:59.032 --> 00:01:01.260 align:middle line:84%
slamming all of our fingers on
the keyboard at once and see

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.860 align:middle line:90%
what happens.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:07.250 align:middle line:84%
And we're kind of looking
for those things.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:11.650 align:middle line:84%
We're also looking for subtler
things like is the amount of

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.342 align:middle line:84%
RAM that those games are taking
up in the computer

00:01:13.342 --> 00:01:17.240 align:middle line:90%
slowly going up all the time?

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:21.090 align:middle line:84%
And of course, comparing it
against a spec grade.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:25.470 align:middle line:84%
So a character is supposed to
be able to create a certain

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.120 align:middle line:84%
file hold style or something
when you enter this key

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:33.865 align:middle line:84%
sequence, and 99 times out of
100 that works and that 1 out

00:01:33.865 --> 00:01:37.110 align:middle line:84%
of 100 doesn't and we need
to figure out why.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:37.670 align:middle line:90%
That's our feat.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:40.920 align:middle line:84%
So in a lot of those situations,
what happens is

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:46.850 align:middle line:84%
that output comes back in the
form of other parts and-- how

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:50.092 align:middle line:84%
many of you actually
do programming?

00:01:50.092 --> 00:01:52.570 align:middle line:90%
Oh, OK.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:55.935 align:middle line:84%
How many of you have found
a book report?

00:01:55.935 --> 00:01:57.753 align:middle line:90%
OK, not that many.

00:01:57.753 --> 00:02:00.770 align:middle line:90%


00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:05.130 align:middle line:84%
There are a couple of best
practices where you follow a

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:08.583 align:middle line:84%
book report and this one
translates a little bit into

00:02:08.583 --> 00:02:11.290 align:middle line:84%
when you're really working
in a team in a game.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:15.430 align:middle line:84%
How do you communicate that
something is wrong to someone

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:18.850 align:middle line:84%
else who is also responsible
for the game, but may not

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:22.500 align:middle line:84%
necessarily have seen
the problem?

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.630 align:middle line:84%
First of all, you describe what
you were expecting to

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:28.550 align:middle line:84%
happen and what you
were doing.

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:32.795 align:middle line:84%
I was expecting to go right when
I push the right button.

00:02:32.795 --> 00:02:34.070 align:middle line:84%
And then you write down
what happened.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:38.410 align:middle line:84%
You try to get as much detail as
possible for the person to

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.230 align:middle line:90%
re-release this problem.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:45.660 align:middle line:84%
Now, when it comes to war games,
in many situations, the

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.480 align:middle line:84%
kind of detail that you need
to provide largely comes up

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:48.720 align:middle line:90%
against it.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.160 align:middle line:84%
So 'a' had this much money, 'b'
had that much money, they

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.010 align:middle line:84%
try to do this game mechanic
and no one knew what to do

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:00.085 align:middle line:84%
because the way the rules are
written there's only one way

00:03:00.085 --> 00:03:02.450 align:middle line:90%
to interpret it.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:06.590 align:middle line:84%
And that gives your team enough
information to be able

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.920 align:middle line:90%
to start acting on it.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:12.150 align:middle line:84%
Because you are bargaining and
your rules are kind of right

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.330 align:middle line:84%
out there in the open, there's
usually less of a mystery to

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:16.810 align:middle line:90%
see where the problem occurs.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.470 align:middle line:84%
But you know there's problem
and it could end and you

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:20.060 align:middle line:90%
largely see where it lies.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.590 align:middle line:84%
You may not know what to do
to correct it, and that's

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.320 align:middle line:84%
actually where beta
testing comes in.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:29.160 align:middle line:84%
So but I'll get to the
testing shortly.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.935 align:middle line:84%
But technical testing, a lot
of it again is comparing a

00:03:32.935 --> 00:03:33.950 align:middle line:90%
given spec.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.382 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes that spec is created
from your team.

00:03:35.382 --> 00:03:36.330 align:middle line:84%
This is what we want
our game to do.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:40.340 align:middle line:84%
This is what we expected to
happen when we made this game.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:44.280 align:middle line:84%
We expect that mid in the game,
a certain game mechanic

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.710 align:middle line:84%
will start to take priority
over other mechanics.

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:51.900 align:middle line:84%
Early on, everyone's buying
property is a monopoly.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:56.990 align:middle line:84%
Later on, everyone's going to
be building houses and then

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:58.910 align:middle line:84%
even at the end, people start
to mortgage stuff.

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:00.920 align:middle line:84%
That's what we kind of
expected out of it.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.630 align:middle line:84%
And then you play the game and
you start realizing you

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.400 align:middle line:84%
mortgaging something way too
early here, something's out of

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:07.700 align:middle line:90%
whack here.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.910 align:middle line:90%
So that's the kind of technical

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:10.780 align:middle line:90%
testing you do in games.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:15.340 align:middle line:84%
This is what we expect, why
isn't it happening?

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:19.940 align:middle line:84%
The other side over here is
kind of like being the

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:23.750 align:middle line:84%
defender of the player, being
the player's advocate.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.360 align:middle line:90%


00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:30.130 align:middle line:84%
How, can you be the person on
the team that is actively

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:34.516 align:middle line:84%
looking for things in your game
that are going to hurt

00:04:34.516 --> 00:04:36.648 align:middle line:84%
your players and then mentioning
it and saying,

00:04:36.648 --> 00:04:38.395 align:middle line:84%
maybe we should do something
about that.

00:04:38.395 --> 00:04:42.810 align:middle line:84%
It's as simple as, here's the
information that we want our

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:44.760 align:middle line:84%
players to have at
a certain time.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.050 align:middle line:90%
Here's the game state.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:49.790 align:middle line:84%
Your characters have
a certain state.

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:52.790 align:middle line:84%
They are this close to winning,
but are this far away

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:55.120 align:middle line:84%
from winning to have
these results here.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.445 align:middle line:84%
Do the players understand
that?

00:04:57.445 --> 00:05:00.730 align:middle line:84%
Have you provided them with
enough information to be able

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:04.340 align:middle line:90%
to make a good decision?

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.145 align:middle line:84%
If they know the game state, do
they know what options they

00:05:06.145 --> 00:05:09.380 align:middle line:90%
have in front of them?

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:13.730 align:middle line:84%
So as we play more and more
board games this semester, you

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:19.620 align:middle line:84%
can actually look at the way
how rules are presented

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:19.870 align:middle line:90%
redundantly.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:23.510 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes they're written up in
the rule sheet, but and of

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:27.400 align:middle line:84%
course you've got your index
card-sized thing which goes

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.270 align:middle line:84%
through step one, two, three,
four, these are the four

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:31.238 align:middle line:84%
things you've got to remember
every single turn.

00:05:31.238 --> 00:05:33.581 align:middle line:84%
You've got to get it, and
everybody has a copy right in

00:05:33.581 --> 00:05:37.000 align:middle line:84%
front of you because somebody on
the team decided that that

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.980 align:middle line:90%
was a good idea.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:42.495 align:middle line:84%
How did they come to
that conclusion?

00:05:42.495 --> 00:05:47.170 align:middle line:84%
Occasionally, they are doing it
because they see there's a

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:48.390 align:middle line:90%
problem right away.

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:52.060 align:middle line:84%
There's too much information,
and you just don't expect any

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:54.690 align:middle line:84%
player is ever going to be
able to grasp all this

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:55.485 align:middle line:90%
information.

00:05:55.485 --> 00:06:00.423 align:middle line:84%
So that's come out with a couple
of ways to simplify it.

00:06:00.423 --> 00:06:03.734 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes that involves taking
up the mechanics, making

00:06:03.734 --> 00:06:07.750 align:middle line:84%
something that was written up a
text graphic report and some

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:09.846 align:middle line:84%
icons, reducing all the numbers
by two so the math

00:06:09.846 --> 00:06:13.060 align:middle line:90%
becomes easier.

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:18.200 align:middle line:84%
But sometimes, you do it by
usability testing, and that's

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:21.260 align:middle line:84%
one kind of testing
that often comes

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:23.640 align:middle line:90%
together with beta testing.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:26.850 align:middle line:84%
I've invited Sara Verrilli, our
development director, to

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:30.709 align:middle line:84%
come in here and talk about the
kind of pre-testing that

00:06:30.709 --> 00:06:35.699 align:middle line:84%
you can do, which is illustrated
by this picture.

00:06:35.699 --> 00:06:38.700 align:middle line:90%


00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:42.500 align:middle line:84%
And the book goes through
several different kinds of

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:45.410 align:middle line:84%
environments where you do
pre-testing that's sitting

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:48.625 align:middle line:84%
one-on-one with people
and doing site

00:06:48.625 --> 00:06:51.320 align:middle line:90%
testing for the group.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:54.422 align:middle line:84%
Because this style is all about
board games and card

00:06:54.422 --> 00:06:57.150 align:middle line:84%
games, for the most part, we're
designing games for

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:58.050 align:middle line:84%
three or four people
to play at a time.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.440 align:middle line:84%
The chances are, almost every
single test that you're going

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.016 align:middle line:84%
to do [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
at a single time.

00:07:03.016 --> 00:07:04.674 align:middle line:84%
That's just the environment
you're in

00:07:04.674 --> 00:07:05.570 align:middle line:90%
that's meant to be played.

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:09.390 align:middle line:84%
That's why you end
up testing it.

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:11.056 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, a single-play
computer game, that's not

00:07:11.056 --> 00:07:13.800 align:middle line:84%
necessarily the environment
you're testing in.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:19.985 align:middle line:84%
Actually, before I transition
over, there is one thing I

00:07:19.985 --> 00:07:21.670 align:middle line:84%
want to mention about
technical testing.

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:23.855 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes the specs you're
testing against are not the

00:07:23.855 --> 00:07:26.710 align:middle line:84%
ones that came up that would
necessarily come up

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:28.640 align:middle line:90%
with phi over t.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:30.520 align:middle line:84%
If you're doing a
computer game--

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:34.510 align:middle line:84%
say for an Xbox or
a PS3 or a Wii.

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:37.001 align:middle line:84%
There are these things called
technical [? evaluation ?]

00:07:37.001 --> 00:07:42.280 align:middle line:84%
requirements, which are
basically huge lists of things

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:46.391 align:middle line:84%
that your game must do before
Microsoft will sign your title

00:07:46.391 --> 00:07:50.720 align:middle line:84%
or allow it to be released
under that title.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:54.796 align:middle line:84%
PC obviously doesn't have this,
but if you release it on

00:07:54.796 --> 00:07:57.900 align:middle line:84%
speed or something, then chances
are, you'll have

00:07:57.900 --> 00:07:58.110 align:middle line:90%
requirements.

00:07:58.110 --> 00:07:59.730 align:middle line:84%
It could be something as
simple as generally, it

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:02.596 align:middle line:90%
doesn't crash--

00:08:02.596 --> 00:08:06.830 align:middle line:84%
and games do crash, even
on PS3 and Xbox--

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:12.330 align:middle line:84%
you get things like what happens
when you're playing a

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:14.442 align:middle line:90%
game on the PS3 or Xbox--

00:08:14.442 --> 00:08:18.012 align:middle line:84%
I guess more the PS2 and the
first Xbox-- and someone yanks

00:08:18.012 --> 00:08:20.880 align:middle line:90%
out the cable?

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:23.510 align:middle line:84%
All you're thinking is that
you're-- and then the keyboard

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:25.805 align:middle line:90%
comes out, what happens?

00:08:25.805 --> 00:08:28.390 align:middle line:90%
Does it continue?

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:29.740 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: It buzzes.

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:31.900 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: It goes into
pause, right?

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:35.289 align:middle line:84%
And usually it tells you, your
controller is disconnected.

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:36.950 align:middle line:90%
And that's a requirement.

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:40.111 align:middle line:84%
Someone had to write that code
specifically to detect that

00:08:40.111 --> 00:08:44.610 align:middle line:84%
situation and then throughout
the suite across the game.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:47.590 align:middle line:84%
And the reason everybody had
to do that was because

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:49.560 align:middle line:84%
Microsoft and Sony
and Nintendo--

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:50.730 align:middle line:84%
well, not so much Nintendo
nowadays

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:53.230 align:middle line:90%
because Nintendo is wireless--

00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:57.880 align:middle line:84%
but these are the kinds of
requirements that they expect.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:01.248 align:middle line:84%
If you lose your connection to
Xbox live halfway through the

00:09:01.248 --> 00:09:03.650 align:middle line:84%
game then something's
going to happen.

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:08.595 align:middle line:84%
And so again, for that kind of
technical testing, you're sort

00:09:08.595 --> 00:09:11.416 align:middle line:84%
of comparing it against spec,
but spec may not necessarily

00:09:11.416 --> 00:09:14.392 align:middle line:84%
be something that you're
keeping up with.

00:09:14.392 --> 00:09:18.860 align:middle line:84%
It may be something that the
publisher or your platform

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:21.230 align:middle line:84%
developer, platform manufacturer
mandated.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:23.750 align:middle line:84%
How does it affect the board
games is that sometimes, board

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:27.225 align:middle line:84%
games are actually going
to require half the

00:09:27.225 --> 00:09:28.480 align:middle line:90%
requirements to that.

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:31.543 align:middle line:84%
If you're responsible for the
design of the box, for

00:09:31.543 --> 00:09:33.930 align:middle line:84%
instance, there are things
they're going to require on

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:38.046 align:middle line:84%
the outside of the box, like
what ages are the game

00:09:38.046 --> 00:09:40.820 align:middle line:84%
applicable for, how long is it
meant to be played, how many

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:43.680 align:middle line:84%
people are going to be
playing your game.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:49.800 align:middle line:84%
I think Days of Wonder, which
is a publisher, actually

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:54.370 align:middle line:84%
requires a character
to be on the box.

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:58.120 align:middle line:84%
Even if your game has no
characters, there must be a

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:01.750 align:middle line:84%
human-like presence on the box
or your players are on the box

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:05.010 align:middle line:84%
because they think that it
increases sales, and they

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:06.720 align:middle line:84%
probably have the numbers
to back it up.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:10.180 align:middle line:84%
That's why if you look at things
like Ticket for Ride,

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:12.168 align:middle line:90%
for instance, has people.

00:10:12.168 --> 00:10:16.450 align:middle line:84%
If you play Ticket to Ride, it
has no people in it, but it

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:19.940 align:middle line:84%
has people on the box because
they want people to think

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:21.335 align:middle line:90%
that's what users are.

00:10:21.335 --> 00:10:25.070 align:middle line:90%


00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:31.397 align:middle line:84%
So that's just an example of
things that a board game

00:10:31.397 --> 00:10:32.790 align:middle line:84%
publisher may require
you to do.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:37.180 align:middle line:84%
Very common that you'll have to
do translations in a range

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:40.920 align:middle line:84%
of different translations for
the different countries it's

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:43.016 align:middle line:90%
going to be released in.

00:10:43.016 --> 00:10:45.435 align:middle line:84%
It may not be your job to do it,
the publishers may end up

00:10:45.435 --> 00:10:47.027 align:middle line:84%
being the ones helping you with
the translation, getting

00:10:47.027 --> 00:10:49.260 align:middle line:84%
all the publishing data
formatted decently, but

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:52.560 align:middle line:84%
someone along the stage is
making sure that happens

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:55.274 align:middle line:84%
before the game hits
the shelf.

00:10:55.274 --> 00:10:58.340 align:middle line:90%


00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:00.618 align:middle line:90%
OK, that's technical testing.

00:11:00.618 --> 00:11:05.956 align:middle line:84%
That's a little bit about
becoming a player and so let's

00:11:05.956 --> 00:11:09.372 align:middle line:90%
talk about data center a bit.

00:11:09.372 --> 00:11:13.764 align:middle line:84%
SARA VERRILLI: Let me bump
you a little here,

00:11:13.764 --> 00:11:14.460 align:middle line:90%
if you don't care.

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:15.710 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Oh, right.

00:11:15.710 --> 00:11:37.466 align:middle line:90%


00:11:37.466 --> 00:11:38.954 align:middle line:90%
SARA VERRILLI: OK.

00:11:38.954 --> 00:11:39.946 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:11:39.946 --> 00:11:41.930 align:middle line:90%
Let's actually go ahead.

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:45.898 align:middle line:90%


00:11:45.898 --> 00:11:47.882 align:middle line:90%
So focus testing.

00:11:47.882 --> 00:11:50.767 align:middle line:84%
This is a very similar track for
the summer program folks.

00:11:50.767 --> 00:11:53.840 align:middle line:84%
I have about 10 minutes to go
back and relook at my notes

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:57.980 align:middle line:84%
and relook at the lecture, so
if it's a little stumbly in

00:11:57.980 --> 00:12:01.209 align:middle line:84%
parts, it's because I haven't
given it for a while and

00:12:01.209 --> 00:12:01.460 align:middle line:90%
haven't thought about it.

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:02.766 align:middle line:84%
It's much more geared, the
original talk is much more

00:12:02.766 --> 00:12:05.140 align:middle line:84%
geared towards computer
testing, and testing

00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:08.890 align:middle line:84%
specifically single-player
computer games than it's

00:12:08.890 --> 00:12:12.770 align:middle line:84%
testing board games, which
are almost a multi-user.

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:16.598 align:middle line:84%
So first of all, focus testing
is actually dissimilar.

00:12:16.598 --> 00:12:20.370 align:middle line:84%
Focus testing is only a specific
case of user testing,

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.720 align:middle line:84%
which is to say, you're testing
your game using actual

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:25.910 align:middle line:84%
users, not your development
team, not people who play

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:28.484 align:middle line:84%
board games 12 hours a day
because that's what they love

00:12:28.484 --> 00:12:31.100 align:middle line:84%
to do all the time, but more the
sort of average person who

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:33.645 align:middle line:84%
might want to pick up your game
off the shelf, go buy it

00:12:33.645 --> 00:12:37.300 align:middle line:84%
and then go play it with their
family and then go out to

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:40.198 align:middle line:84%
dinner at Chuck E. Cheese
or something.

00:12:40.198 --> 00:12:44.245 align:middle line:84%
Another type of user tester is
usability testing, which is

00:12:44.245 --> 00:12:46.770 align:middle line:84%
fairly similar to focus testing
in as much as you're

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:48.360 align:middle line:90%
still using users for it.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:50.860 align:middle line:84%
Focus testing is usually much
more geared towards are people

00:12:50.860 --> 00:12:52.073 align:middle line:90%
enjoying your game?

00:12:52.073 --> 00:12:55.370 align:middle line:84%
Do people want to
play your game?

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:59.060 align:middle line:84%
Are people going through the
sort of play experiences you

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:01.290 align:middle line:84%
want them to have as they
play their game?

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:02.920 align:middle line:84%
Because usually when you
create a game, you're

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:05.410 align:middle line:84%
thinking, oh, like for Settlers
of Catan, you expect

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:06.950 align:middle line:84%
people to spend a lot of time
talking with each other

00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:09.326 align:middle line:90%
trading things back and forth.

00:13:09.326 --> 00:13:12.960 align:middle line:84%
When you're making Diplomacy,
you expect people to spend a

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:16.200 align:middle line:84%
lot of time arguing with each
other over well, if you invade

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:19.060 align:middle line:84%
that country, I'll invade that
country and we'll gang up on

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:21.280 align:middle line:84%
him and we'll all come out of
the game and then we'll just

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:25.098 align:middle line:84%
divide four between the two of
us and win while you're making

00:13:25.098 --> 00:13:25.618 align:middle line:84%
secret underhanded arrangements
with the other

00:13:25.618 --> 00:13:28.000 align:middle line:84%
guys, but OK, so he's going to
attack you on this ground, we

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:32.330 align:middle line:84%
both know that, that sort of
thing, as opposed to, say,

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:35.192 align:middle line:84%
Transamerica which is just more
putting pieces down and

00:13:35.192 --> 00:13:38.420 align:middle line:84%
it's pretty much independent
play in many ways.

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:41.420 align:middle line:90%


00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:45.612 align:middle line:84%
Usability testing is much more
about how you easy is it for

00:13:45.612 --> 00:13:46.920 align:middle line:90%
people to play your game.

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:49.150 align:middle line:90%
Do they understand your rules?

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:50.490 align:middle line:90%
Are the mechanics clear?

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:53.100 align:middle line:84%
When they sit down and start
sorting out the tokens, can

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:57.055 align:middle line:84%
they tell which tokens are the
player pieces and which cards

00:13:57.055 --> 00:13:58.056 align:middle line:84%
are which and how
you use those?

00:13:58.056 --> 00:14:01.220 align:middle line:84%
Do the icons on your game
make sense to people?

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:02.570 align:middle line:90%
Do they understand them?

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:03.020 align:middle line:90%
Things like that.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:06.162 align:middle line:84%
It's not really about whether
or not people like your game

00:14:06.162 --> 00:14:07.400 align:middle line:90%
or are enjoying your game.

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:11.056 align:middle line:84%
It's about can people understand
your game and start

00:14:11.056 --> 00:14:12.306 align:middle line:90%
playing it.

00:14:12.306 --> 00:14:14.500 align:middle line:90%


00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:19.545 align:middle line:90%
So here we go.

00:14:19.545 --> 00:14:23.703 align:middle line:84%
One big problem we run into when
people sit down and we

00:14:23.703 --> 00:14:25.385 align:middle line:84%
have people playing your game
and giving lots of comments,

00:14:25.385 --> 00:14:27.670 align:middle line:84%
this was really fun or this
really sucked, or I hate this

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:29.080 align:middle line:84%
part where I have to spend
time talking to the other

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:33.120 align:middle line:84%
players, I want to be able to
stare at just my cards and

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:36.350 align:middle line:84%
board completely without
interacting with anyone else.

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:37.710 align:middle line:84%
Focus testers will give you
lots and lots of feedback,

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:41.140 align:middle line:84%
some of it is useful,
some of it is not.

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:45.986 align:middle line:84%
And you need to remember that
focus testers are not actually

00:14:45.986 --> 00:14:46.890 align:middle line:90%
people building the game.

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:47.740 align:middle line:84%
Focus testers are the people
who you are trying to get

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:52.020 align:middle line:84%
information about your game from
and one problem that we

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:56.890 align:middle line:84%
often see in experienced game
developers and people making

00:14:56.890 --> 00:15:01.560 align:middle line:84%
games doing is, oh, the focus
tester said we have to make

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:06.840 align:middle line:84%
the whole game red, quick, let's
make everything red.

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:11.180 align:middle line:84%
So and that's usually,
that's not a very

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:11.957 align:middle line:90%
good solution, right?

00:15:11.957 --> 00:15:13.780 align:middle line:84%
Who knows what the problem with
this actually was, but

00:15:13.780 --> 00:15:16.970 align:middle line:84%
what you need to do is find out
why did the players want

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:18.010 align:middle line:90%
to make everything red?

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:19.550 align:middle line:84%
Is it because they don't
like the artistic

00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:21.858 align:middle line:90%
design of the game?

00:15:21.858 --> 00:15:22.340 align:middle line:84%
Is it because someone in
the game is colorblind?

00:15:22.340 --> 00:15:26.196 align:middle line:84%
Someone playing the game
currently is colorblind, is

00:15:26.196 --> 00:15:29.088 align:middle line:84%
having a hard time telling
things apart so they'd like

00:15:29.088 --> 00:15:31.304 align:middle line:84%
everything to be the same color
so everyone's having the

00:15:31.304 --> 00:15:32.275 align:middle line:90%
same problem they are.

00:15:32.275 --> 00:15:33.250 align:middle line:90%
I mean, who knows?

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:37.720 align:middle line:84%
It's hard to tell what
people are thinking.

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:40.320 align:middle line:84%
When they give you advice, and
when they give you these

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:41.560 align:middle line:84%
comments, you have to have
some way to filter those

00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:44.260 align:middle line:84%
comments, some way to think
about them and some way to

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:48.500 align:middle line:84%
decide what actually is the
right reaction to take in view

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:52.525 align:middle line:84%
of what you want your game to do
and how you want people to

00:15:52.525 --> 00:15:52.660 align:middle line:90%
play the game.

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:54.887 align:middle line:84%
Since you're the game designers
and developers, you

00:15:54.887 --> 00:15:57.100 align:middle line:90%
have to make those decisions.

00:15:57.100 --> 00:16:00.014 align:middle line:84%
Focus testers can only
give you data.

00:16:00.014 --> 00:16:02.882 align:middle line:84%
You shouldn't ask them to design
your game for you.

00:16:02.882 --> 00:16:05.750 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you actually have good
ideas that you want to use,

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:08.316 align:middle line:84%
but you want to make sure that
they're good ideas that you do

00:16:08.316 --> 00:16:10.800 align:middle line:90%
want to use.

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:16.866 align:middle line:84%
So you may notice, these are
both my lectures, and these

00:16:16.866 --> 00:16:20.170 align:middle line:84%
are pictures that Mike
[? Dropho ?] drew for a

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:21.720 align:middle line:84%
poster, and we love
him so much.

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:25.150 align:middle line:84%
So in the ideal focus testing
situation, you're really

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:29.090 align:middle line:84%
trying to get as much data as
you can about your player

00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:32.765 align:middle line:84%
playing the game without
spoiling the information.

00:16:32.765 --> 00:16:36.060 align:middle line:84%
What you'd really like to
recreate in a lot of ways is,

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:37.826 align:middle line:84%
so you just bought
the game and have

00:16:37.826 --> 00:16:38.610 align:middle line:90%
gotten home, what happens?

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:43.100 align:middle line:84%
And that's what this picture is
all about, there's somebody

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:47.240 align:middle line:84%
luring somebody to play the game
without any information.

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:53.117 align:middle line:90%


00:16:53.117 --> 00:17:03.995 align:middle line:84%
So things to think about when
you're planning your focus

00:17:03.995 --> 00:17:09.520 align:middle line:84%
testing is, what are
you testing?

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:11.900 align:middle line:84%
What questions do
you want to ask?

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:13.328 align:middle line:84%
What information do you
want to get out of it?

00:17:13.328 --> 00:17:15.940 align:middle line:90%
How are you going to test it?

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:18.490 align:middle line:90%
How many players do you need?

00:17:18.490 --> 00:17:21.170 align:middle line:84%
What kind of interactions
do you want to see?

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:24.554 align:middle line:84%
On the backhand, who are
you testing with?

00:17:24.554 --> 00:17:26.582 align:middle line:84%
What kind of people are going
to make your game?

00:17:26.582 --> 00:17:30.912 align:middle line:84%
Are you making a game for
children age 9 to 15?

00:17:30.912 --> 00:17:33.407 align:middle line:84%
Are you making it for
college-age students?

00:17:33.407 --> 00:17:37.648 align:middle line:84%
Are you making a game that you
think that older players will

00:17:37.648 --> 00:17:38.896 align:middle line:90%
want to play?

00:17:38.896 --> 00:17:41.391 align:middle line:84%
Because if you're making a game
that you think is going

00:17:41.391 --> 00:17:43.387 align:middle line:84%
to be popular with the senior
citizens market, testing it

00:17:43.387 --> 00:17:45.882 align:middle line:84%
with a bunch of elementary
school kids probably isn't

00:17:45.882 --> 00:17:48.876 align:middle line:90%
going to go very will.

00:17:48.876 --> 00:17:52.312 align:middle line:84%
Trivial Pursuit Classic Edition,
a game that came out

00:17:52.312 --> 00:17:56.130 align:middle line:84%
about 20-odd years ago, I
suspect that if you sat down a

00:17:56.130 --> 00:18:00.082 align:middle line:84%
group of high school students
with it and had them play it,

00:18:00.082 --> 00:18:00.786 align:middle line:84%
about three quarters of the
questions they'd just stare at

00:18:00.786 --> 00:18:05.022 align:middle line:84%
you and be like, I've
never heard of that.

00:18:05.022 --> 00:18:09.468 align:middle line:84%
So, that's certainly where
the audience comes in.

00:18:09.468 --> 00:18:14.408 align:middle line:84%
So, the what, planning out the
questions that you think

00:18:14.408 --> 00:18:15.396 align:middle line:84%
you're going to get
information for.

00:18:15.396 --> 00:18:19.350 align:middle line:84%
When you know what information
for focus testing, it's a lot

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:22.630 align:middle line:84%
easier to know what to keep
track of, how to take notes,

00:18:22.630 --> 00:18:25.981 align:middle line:84%
what reactions you're most
interested in as people are

00:18:25.981 --> 00:18:27.238 align:middle line:90%
playing the game.

00:18:27.238 --> 00:18:30.136 align:middle line:84%
So, what questions are
you going to ask?

00:18:30.136 --> 00:18:32.080 align:middle line:84%
What data it takes to answer
your question?

00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:33.930 align:middle line:84%
And how are you going
to get that data?

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:35.220 align:middle line:90%
Are you going to go all through

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:36.780 align:middle line:90%
observation taking notes?

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:38.620 align:middle line:84%
Are you going to just step
in and out of questions?

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:42.249 align:middle line:84%
So why did you move that
character from there, just out

00:18:42.249 --> 00:18:43.873 align:middle line:84%
of curiosity I'm wondering
why you made that move.

00:18:43.873 --> 00:18:46.308 align:middle line:90%
Will you tell me about it?

00:18:46.308 --> 00:18:49.230 align:middle line:84%
Or, you can ask them to fill
out a questionnaire

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:49.717 align:middle line:90%
at the end of it.

00:18:49.717 --> 00:18:53.126 align:middle line:84%
Depending on what data
you want, different

00:18:53.126 --> 00:18:54.610 align:middle line:90%
methods will work best.

00:18:54.610 --> 00:19:01.684 align:middle line:84%
In general, usually the best way
to do it is observation.

00:19:01.684 --> 00:19:05.636 align:middle line:84%
As soon as you ask someone
questions, you sort of perturb

00:19:05.636 --> 00:19:08.366 align:middle line:84%
the system and when you ask
them to fill out a survey,

00:19:08.366 --> 00:19:09.930 align:middle line:90%
it's after they've done it.

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:12.030 align:middle line:84%
They've kind of forgotten
what they were doing.

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:14.520 align:middle line:84%
They may not exactly remember,
and to be perfectly honest,

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:15.530 align:middle line:84%
people would really rather
play your game

00:19:15.530 --> 00:19:17.140 align:middle line:90%
than fill out a form.

00:19:17.140 --> 00:19:21.650 align:middle line:84%
So they'll be more engaged
more interested and more

00:19:21.650 --> 00:19:24.606 align:middle line:84%
interactive when they're playing
a game and that's when

00:19:24.606 --> 00:19:25.770 align:middle line:84%
you're going to get
your best data.

00:19:25.770 --> 00:19:28.190 align:middle line:84%
But there are times when you
want to ask someone something

00:19:28.190 --> 00:19:29.920 align:middle line:84%
sort of analytical and you
don't want someone to be

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:32.910 align:middle line:84%
thinking analytically while
they're playing the game in a

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:35.614 align:middle line:84%
lot of ways , and so there are
times when that reflection

00:19:35.614 --> 00:19:39.125 align:middle line:84%
comes out so much better and
it's better to have that

00:19:39.125 --> 00:19:40.320 align:middle line:84%
written down and get it
in your own words.

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:44.505 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: You would hope it plays
better than a survey.

00:19:44.505 --> 00:19:46.920 align:middle line:84%
SARA VERRILLI: If your game is
better than a survey, you

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:51.358 align:middle line:84%
probably already observed a
problem right there and you're

00:19:51.358 --> 00:19:53.310 align:middle line:90%
probably glad you found it.

00:19:53.310 --> 00:19:57.230 align:middle line:90%


00:19:57.230 --> 00:19:58.390 align:middle line:90%
So how?

00:19:58.390 --> 00:20:01.030 align:middle line:84%
In order to get all that data,
you're going to have to

00:20:01.030 --> 00:20:03.250 align:middle line:84%
actually interact with the user,
someone who's never seen

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:06.300 align:middle line:84%
your game before, someone who
doesn't actually care whether

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:08.550 align:middle line:84%
or not you get good
information out of

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:09.250 align:middle line:90%
playing this game.

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:11.330 align:middle line:84%
They came here to sit down and
play the game, and while they

00:20:11.330 --> 00:20:14.260 align:middle line:84%
would like to help you by giving
you information, that's

00:20:14.260 --> 00:20:17.030 align:middle line:84%
not their primary goal
in a lot of ways.

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:19.074 align:middle line:84%
Usually, their primary goal is
to sit down, and play the

00:20:19.074 --> 00:20:19.190 align:middle line:90%
game, and have a good time.

00:20:19.190 --> 00:20:21.610 align:middle line:84%
So you need to be able to
structure the interaction with

00:20:21.610 --> 00:20:25.210 align:middle line:84%
them so that getting the
data to you is as

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:27.310 align:middle line:90%
easy for them as possible.

00:20:27.310 --> 00:20:30.115 align:middle line:84%
So we need to think about what
this player needs before he or

00:20:30.115 --> 00:20:30.740 align:middle line:90%
she sits down to play.

00:20:30.740 --> 00:20:32.464 align:middle line:84%
Usually, in a board game
or a card game,

00:20:32.464 --> 00:20:33.990 align:middle line:90%
that's the set of rules.

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:36.520 align:middle line:84%
In a computer, it's
always trickier.

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:39.700 align:middle line:84%
Usually when we're bringing in
people to test the first

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:42.690 align:middle line:84%
prototypes, we have a game that
works, but we don't have

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:43.490 align:middle line:90%
any instructions in game.

00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:45.390 align:middle line:84%
If we're going to have a
tutorial or anything like

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:48.080 align:middle line:84%
that, that's usually one of the
last things that goes in.

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:50.620 align:middle line:84%
So when you're testing computer
games early in the

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:53.170 align:middle line:84%
process, usually you end up
sitting down and writing out,

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:54.820 align:middle line:84%
well, this is what the tutorial
would look like if we

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:57.120 align:middle line:84%
had it, so read this and then
sit down and play it.

00:20:57.120 --> 00:20:59.005 align:middle line:84%
With board games and card games,
it's actually a little

00:20:59.005 --> 00:21:01.610 align:middle line:84%
bit easier, because you can say,
a ha, this is the rules

00:21:01.610 --> 00:21:03.530 align:middle line:84%
that are going to package
with the games.

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:05.810 align:middle line:84%
Give you the rules, step back
and see what happens.

00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:07.920 align:middle line:84%
So, it's actually an
easier aspect for

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:10.330 align:middle line:90%
testing board games.

00:21:10.330 --> 00:21:14.636 align:middle line:84%
And finally, treat the
players politely.

00:21:14.636 --> 00:21:16.995 align:middle line:84%
When they find a misspelling
in your rules or when they

00:21:16.995 --> 00:21:20.050 align:middle line:84%
discover that you've left out
six of the key cards to play

00:21:20.050 --> 00:21:23.005 align:middle line:84%
the game, oh, yeah, you're
right, thank you for pointing

00:21:23.005 --> 00:21:25.868 align:middle line:84%
that out, I wouldn't have
noticed that if you hadn't

00:21:25.868 --> 00:21:26.260 align:middle line:90%
shown it to me.

00:21:26.260 --> 00:21:28.255 align:middle line:84%
Whether you would have or not
doesn't really matter.

00:21:28.255 --> 00:21:30.490 align:middle line:84%
They're doing you a favor by
playing your game that isn't

00:21:30.490 --> 00:21:33.460 align:middle line:84%
quite working well, so you want
them to know that their

00:21:33.460 --> 00:21:37.557 align:middle line:84%
contribution is invaluable and
you appreciate the time and

00:21:37.557 --> 00:21:38.807 align:middle line:90%
effort they've put into it.

00:21:38.807 --> 00:21:42.850 align:middle line:90%


00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:46.110 align:middle line:84%
A few things, the more they talk
about your game and the

00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:48.390 align:middle line:84%
mechanics they find that break
or that do or don't work,

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:51.482 align:middle line:84%
that's all data right there,
That's all great.

00:21:51.482 --> 00:21:54.077 align:middle line:84%
Another great advantage of
testing board games is you

00:21:54.077 --> 00:21:56.240 align:middle line:84%
usually get groups of players,
like two or three or four

00:21:56.240 --> 00:21:59.520 align:middle line:84%
players, and when you have
multiple players, they'll talk

00:21:59.520 --> 00:22:00.526 align:middle line:90%
to each other about it.

00:22:00.526 --> 00:22:01.080 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I noticed this.

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:03.390 align:middle line:90%
Did you notice this strategy?

00:22:03.390 --> 00:22:05.115 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I did, but I noticed that
if you add that strategy

00:22:05.115 --> 00:22:07.780 align:middle line:84%
that you can do this thing and
this other thing and that's

00:22:07.780 --> 00:22:09.240 align:middle line:90%
all really neat.

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:10.926 align:middle line:84%
When you're testing
single-player computer games

00:22:10.926 --> 00:22:13.940 align:middle line:84%
like we usually do in the summer
program, you've got a

00:22:13.940 --> 00:22:15.990 align:middle line:84%
single user that is sitting
there between

00:22:15.990 --> 00:22:17.020 align:middle line:90%
them and the computer.

00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:19.735 align:middle line:84%
You have to watch and figure
out what they're thinking.

00:22:19.735 --> 00:22:22.776 align:middle line:84%
When you have a group, it's not
usually that hard to get

00:22:22.776 --> 00:22:24.553 align:middle line:84%
them talking about what they're
doing and what they're

00:22:24.553 --> 00:22:27.347 align:middle line:84%
thinking, and so that's a
really good way to try

00:22:27.347 --> 00:22:29.073 align:middle line:90%
and get more data.

00:22:29.073 --> 00:22:34.020 align:middle line:90%


00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:38.215 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, here's sort
of the end summary.

00:22:38.215 --> 00:22:40.100 align:middle line:84%
Have a specific question
and questions in mind.

00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:43.380 align:middle line:84%
Know what you're doing in focus
testing, know why you're

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:44.330 align:middle line:90%
doing usability testing.

00:22:44.330 --> 00:22:48.150 align:middle line:84%
If you don't know what you're
trying to test for, then your

00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:50.370 align:middle line:84%
data is going to be a
lot harder to figure

00:22:50.370 --> 00:22:51.220 align:middle line:90%
out what to do with.

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:53.810 align:middle line:84%
So you always want to we have
that question in mind.

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:55.250 align:middle line:84%
That's actually a
really big one.

00:22:55.250 --> 00:22:59.974 align:middle line:84%
What data you need to answer
that question so you can

00:22:59.974 --> 00:23:03.040 align:middle line:84%
collect it and know how to
collect it, and make sure that

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:05.750 align:middle line:84%
you can give the information to
the players, a standardized

00:23:05.750 --> 00:23:08.925 align:middle line:84%
set of information to the
players and step away so

00:23:08.925 --> 00:23:11.200 align:middle line:84%
you're not sort of coloring
the information and giving

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:13.770 align:middle line:84%
them the things you're talking
about by, oh I forgot to

00:23:13.770 --> 00:23:17.290 align:middle line:84%
mention, oh and did I say, so
you've actually got sort of a

00:23:17.290 --> 00:23:23.635 align:middle line:84%
standardized standard size play
group scenario set down.

00:23:23.635 --> 00:23:26.605 align:middle line:84%
OK, I think that's actually
what [? I came for. ?]

00:23:26.605 --> 00:23:29.580 align:middle line:90%


00:23:29.580 --> 00:23:33.496 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So a couple of
words about when to be

00:23:33.496 --> 00:23:37.104 align:middle line:84%
testing, which is really
as soon as possible.

00:23:37.104 --> 00:23:40.540 align:middle line:84%
Usually, before you think it's
possible, you should be

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:44.780 align:middle line:84%
already starting to plan your
fist test because at that

00:23:44.780 --> 00:23:47.228 align:middle line:84%
point, you're probably going for
the lowest-hanging fruit,

00:23:47.228 --> 00:23:50.216 align:middle line:84%
which will be probably somebody
else in this class

00:23:50.216 --> 00:23:53.900 align:middle line:84%
because they're going to give
you feedback anyways, you can

00:23:53.900 --> 00:23:56.340 align:middle line:90%
use that favor in exchange.

00:23:56.340 --> 00:23:59.480 align:middle line:84%
The whole point of making
testing as simple as possible

00:23:59.480 --> 00:24:02.830 align:middle line:84%
is you want to be able to get
information while you still

00:24:02.830 --> 00:24:08.550 align:middle line:84%
have time to fix it, to do
something with that data.

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:12.050 align:middle line:84%
What has happened a lot of times
in this class is we have

00:24:12.050 --> 00:24:15.650 align:middle line:84%
a project team where they come
up with a game requires say,

00:24:15.650 --> 00:24:20.100 align:middle line:84%
generating so many cards or that
they're going to spend

00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:23.150 align:middle line:84%
most of the project deadline
actually creating those cards.

00:24:23.150 --> 00:24:26.710 align:middle line:84%
They'll test it, but they'll
test it on themselves because

00:24:26.710 --> 00:24:28.830 align:middle line:84%
the team already gets
it and they're not

00:24:28.830 --> 00:24:29.720 align:middle line:90%
looking for other people.

00:24:29.720 --> 00:24:34.696 align:middle line:84%
They have a plan to ask their
friends to come in for a

00:24:34.696 --> 00:24:35.374 align:middle line:84%
specific time, they've
set aside an hour or

00:24:35.374 --> 00:24:36.330 align:middle line:90%
something like that.

00:24:36.330 --> 00:24:39.200 align:middle line:84%
So what happens is that the
last week comes around and

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:41.270 align:middle line:84%
they start playing it with
people who haven't seen the

00:24:41.270 --> 00:24:44.020 align:middle line:84%
game before and then discovered
problems.

00:24:44.020 --> 00:24:45.970 align:middle line:84%
Unfortunately, the game
has 100 cards that all

00:24:45.970 --> 00:24:48.350 align:middle line:90%
now need to be fixed.

00:24:48.350 --> 00:24:50.540 align:middle line:84%
This is a problem because
you just discovered

00:24:50.540 --> 00:24:52.826 align:middle line:90%
that problem too late.

00:24:52.826 --> 00:24:54.730 align:middle line:84%
A couple of ways to handle
that, though, one, don't

00:24:54.730 --> 00:24:59.236 align:middle line:84%
decide on a game where making
changes across the entire game

00:24:59.236 --> 00:25:02.140 align:middle line:84%
is going to take you more time
than to test it because that's

00:25:02.140 --> 00:25:04.820 align:middle line:84%
going to make it impossible
to fix anything.

00:25:04.820 --> 00:25:06.910 align:middle line:84%
But the other one is
just testing early.

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:10.335 align:middle line:84%
As soon as you've got a couple
of key mechanics down, sure

00:25:10.335 --> 00:25:12.580 align:middle line:84%
you are planning to play
this game with 100

00:25:12.580 --> 00:25:15.360 align:middle line:90%
cards, start with 20.

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:19.150 align:middle line:84%
Start with a small of group of
cards, hence your core can see

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:20.540 align:middle line:84%
people and understand that
they're going to discover

00:25:20.540 --> 00:25:24.190 align:middle line:84%
things in the production of
those 20 cards and in the

00:25:24.190 --> 00:25:27.160 align:middle line:84%
testing of those 20 cards that
maybe you don't want to be

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:31.790 align:middle line:84%
doing for the next test and you
can redo those 20 cards

00:25:31.790 --> 00:25:33.930 align:middle line:84%
pretty quickly the way that
you can't redo 100 cards.

00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:35.280 align:middle line:90%
Just an example.

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:38.530 align:middle line:84%
I'm really not encouraging
people to do a 100-card game,

00:25:38.530 --> 00:25:39.805 align:middle line:90%
by the way.

00:25:39.805 --> 00:25:41.986 align:middle line:84%
A regular poker deck, 52 cards,
that's all you need for

00:25:41.986 --> 00:25:43.236 align:middle line:90%
a really interesting game.

00:25:43.236 --> 00:25:45.600 align:middle line:90%


00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:47.760 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:25:47.760 --> 00:25:50.140 align:middle line:90%
Some other things.

00:25:50.140 --> 00:25:53.520 align:middle line:84%
Say you're doing a test,
but it's not with

00:25:53.520 --> 00:25:54.880 align:middle line:90%
your entire team present.

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:59.620 align:middle line:84%
Say it's just you and your
teammates are all living in

00:25:59.620 --> 00:26:02.700 align:middle line:84%
different dorms and you
decided to play with

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:03.120 align:middle line:90%
those in your dorm.

00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:05.370 align:middle line:84%
That's great, because it's a
cool game and you've got your

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:08.060 align:middle line:84%
feedback, how do you
communicate that

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:09.020 align:middle line:90%
back to your team?

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:11.890 align:middle line:84%
I talked a little bit about
writing up a little report,

00:26:11.890 --> 00:26:14.976 align:middle line:84%
but here's a couple of other
tips because one thing you

00:26:14.976 --> 00:26:17.520 align:middle line:84%
have to remember is that chances
are, every single idea

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:19.400 align:middle line:84%
in the game, somebody in your
team is probably really

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:22.970 align:middle line:84%
passionate about it and what
you're basically trying to do

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:25.733 align:middle line:84%
is try to convince someone in
your team that maybe this idea

00:26:25.733 --> 00:26:27.550 align:middle line:90%
should be changed.

00:26:27.550 --> 00:26:30.370 align:middle line:84%
And your game will probably be
better for it, but there are

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:33.470 align:middle line:84%
some tactful was of
telling that.

00:26:33.470 --> 00:26:36.900 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:39.680 align:middle line:84%
Keep it short, this also applies
to book reports.

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:42.580 align:middle line:84%
I talked about you giving people
enough information to

00:26:42.580 --> 00:26:46.710 align:middle line:84%
reproduce what the problem was
so that they can understand

00:26:46.710 --> 00:26:49.940 align:middle line:84%
the state of the game and why
the problem is occurring.

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:53.380 align:middle line:84%
And the flip side of that is not
too much information, not

00:26:53.380 --> 00:26:56.320 align:middle line:84%
more information than is
necessary to keep it short.

00:26:56.320 --> 00:26:59.870 align:middle line:84%
Little [? farm ?] codes, give
them something that's easy for

00:26:59.870 --> 00:27:01.220 align:middle line:90%
people to scan.

00:27:01.220 --> 00:27:04.149 align:middle line:84%
Don't make them read and essay
for them to figure out what

00:27:04.149 --> 00:27:06.250 align:middle line:90%
comments will be solved.

00:27:06.250 --> 00:27:08.610 align:middle line:84%
Now actually, one thing to keep
in mind and if you keep

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:12.060 align:middle line:84%
in mind and think about what
Sara said earlier about the

00:27:12.060 --> 00:27:14.470 align:middle line:84%
data that you decide to collect,
one problem with

00:27:14.470 --> 00:27:16.850 align:middle line:84%
board games is with all the
interesting conversations

00:27:16.850 --> 00:27:19.960 align:middle line:84%
happening around the table and
multiple players suggesting

00:27:19.960 --> 00:27:22.992 align:middle line:84%
strategies to each other, you
can come up with reams of

00:27:22.992 --> 00:27:24.390 align:middle line:84%
notes in a single play
test session.

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:29.180 align:middle line:84%
How do you decide to digest
that is really important.

00:27:29.180 --> 00:27:33.735 align:middle line:84%
And you're the only person
there and you're the only

00:27:33.735 --> 00:27:35.611 align:middle line:84%
person on your team, you need
to try to let the rest

00:27:35.611 --> 00:27:36.840 align:middle line:90%
of your team know.

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:39.050 align:middle line:84%
You need to put in the work
of digesting all of that

00:27:39.050 --> 00:27:43.860 align:middle line:84%
information before necessarily
giving it over to your team.

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:47.780 align:middle line:84%
SARA VERRILLI: One possible
solution also is you can get

00:27:47.780 --> 00:27:52.680 align:middle line:84%
permission from play testers
for a tape recorder.

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:56.476 align:middle line:84%
Just get a digital recorder
and record all of the

00:27:56.476 --> 00:27:59.060 align:middle line:90%
conversation as it goes on.

00:27:59.060 --> 00:28:01.596 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: But you still want
to pick out the keys, you

00:28:01.596 --> 00:28:04.090 align:middle line:84%
don't want to revisit the
entire conversation.

00:28:04.090 --> 00:28:07.873 align:middle line:84%
Because if the game took an
hour, and every single one of

00:28:07.873 --> 00:28:11.240 align:middle line:84%
your team members visits, by now
you've lost three hours.

00:28:11.240 --> 00:28:14.350 align:middle line:84%
So you want to find that, do
that little bit of work to

00:28:14.350 --> 00:28:16.580 align:middle line:90%
save yourself the detail.

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:19.570 align:middle line:84%
But that's the way because you
can focus, you can take down

00:28:19.570 --> 00:28:23.110 align:middle line:84%
notes on the main points and
just let the tape recorder

00:28:23.110 --> 00:28:26.370 align:middle line:84%
record all the details so that
you don't have to worry about

00:28:26.370 --> 00:28:29.295 align:middle line:84%
recording and really, the main
points are the things that you

00:28:29.295 --> 00:28:30.130 align:middle line:84%
want to make sure you get
across to your team.

00:28:30.130 --> 00:28:32.526 align:middle line:84%
The tape recorder is
just evidence.

00:28:32.526 --> 00:28:37.460 align:middle line:84%
You could use video depending on
who you're recording with.

00:28:37.460 --> 00:28:40.604 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes people get kind
of antsy when they

00:28:40.604 --> 00:28:41.880 align:middle line:90%
know there's a camera.

00:28:41.880 --> 00:28:43.109 align:middle line:90%
That being said--

00:28:43.109 --> 00:28:48.099 align:middle line:84%
SARA VERRILLI: And also, just
put the video tilted at the

00:28:48.099 --> 00:28:48.941 align:middle line:84%
board so you're not recording
the players, you're

00:28:48.941 --> 00:28:49.097 align:middle line:90%
recording the game.

00:28:49.097 --> 00:28:50.842 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Actually webcams
are really good for

00:28:50.842 --> 00:28:51.093 align:middle line:90%
that sort of thing.

00:28:51.093 --> 00:28:51.592 align:middle line:90%
SARA VERRILLI: Yeah.

00:28:51.592 --> 00:28:55.782 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: You don't run out of
tape, just record straight

00:28:55.782 --> 00:28:56.083 align:middle line:90%
to hard drive.

00:28:56.083 --> 00:28:56.582 align:middle line:84%
SARA VERRILLI: Easy to fast
forward through, also.

00:28:56.582 --> 00:29:00.740 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Yeah, with
your editing tools.

00:29:00.740 --> 00:29:02.560 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:29:02.560 --> 00:29:05.820 align:middle line:84%
So when you're reporting to
other people, think like

00:29:05.820 --> 00:29:08.640 align:middle line:90%
bullet points for instance.

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:13.410 align:middle line:84%
Huge long paragraphs of
prose, not so much.

00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:14.490 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:29:14.490 --> 00:29:16.930 align:middle line:84%
Try to avoid using the following
words, broken,

00:29:16.930 --> 00:29:25.000 align:middle line:84%
problem, unusable, confusing,
kind of odd, you can get more

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:27.680 align:middle line:84%
specific than that, but these
are words that kind of like

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:31.150 align:middle line:84%
imply not only is there a
problem, someone made a

00:29:31.150 --> 00:29:34.900 align:middle line:84%
mistake in his design
of the game.

00:29:34.900 --> 00:29:39.589 align:middle line:84%
And truth be told, most of the
time it wasn't a mistake, you

00:29:39.589 --> 00:29:41.923 align:middle line:84%
just didn't have the complete
play testing, you probably

00:29:41.923 --> 00:29:42.740 align:middle line:90%
just had the designer.

00:29:42.740 --> 00:29:44.575 align:middle line:84%
But you don't want to make
someone feel like they've made

00:29:44.575 --> 00:29:49.060 align:middle line:84%
a mistake because that makes
them defensive and then

00:29:49.060 --> 00:29:51.814 align:middle line:84%
they're going to try to defend
how what they wrote in the

00:29:51.814 --> 00:29:55.230 align:middle line:84%
first place is actually correct,
which is actually not

00:29:55.230 --> 00:29:56.480 align:middle line:90%
correct either.

00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:58.850 align:middle line:90%


00:29:58.850 --> 00:30:01.934 align:middle line:84%
You can talk about features
that need attention.

00:30:01.934 --> 00:30:06.774 align:middle line:84%
With attention, this feature
could be confused.

00:30:06.774 --> 00:30:10.270 align:middle line:84%
I guess that's actually
on the edge there.

00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:18.722 align:middle line:84%
So but just generally, pick your
words carefully and one

00:30:18.722 --> 00:30:23.024 align:middle line:84%
thing that Sara mentioned
earlier was that testers are

00:30:23.024 --> 00:30:26.080 align:middle line:84%
not designers and key designers
are actually not a

00:30:26.080 --> 00:30:27.397 align:middle line:90%
good testing team.

00:30:27.397 --> 00:30:31.728 align:middle line:84%
So for the most part, you are
immediately going to exclude

00:30:31.728 --> 00:30:34.737 align:middle line:84%
yourself from your target
audience because you know too

00:30:34.737 --> 00:30:35.010 align:middle line:90%
much of the game.

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:36.590 align:middle line:84%
This is also covered
in your book.

00:30:36.590 --> 00:30:39.210 align:middle line:84%
As soon as you've played through
your game once or

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:40.290 align:middle line:90%
twice, that's it.

00:30:40.290 --> 00:30:41.800 align:middle line:90%
Your kind of work is finished.

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:46.626 align:middle line:84%
You can still be a good
technical tester, I guess.

00:30:46.626 --> 00:30:49.914 align:middle line:84%
You can still check whether some
mechanics are working the

00:30:49.914 --> 00:30:53.240 align:middle line:84%
way that you want to work, but
you can't tell whether the

00:30:53.240 --> 00:30:55.232 align:middle line:84%
dynamics in your settings
are working anymore.

00:30:55.232 --> 00:30:58.718 align:middle line:84%
You have to get your
focus testing done.

00:30:58.718 --> 00:31:02.204 align:middle line:84%
SARA VERRILLI: You also really
can't tell, you can never

00:31:02.204 --> 00:31:05.690 align:middle line:84%
tell-- you can never share
your usability tester.

00:31:05.690 --> 00:31:07.184 align:middle line:84%
It will always make
sense to you.

00:31:07.184 --> 00:31:10.504 align:middle line:84%
It's amazing that what makes
sense to you is completely

00:31:10.504 --> 00:31:14.156 align:middle line:84%
incomprehensible to the very
intelligent person you give

00:31:14.156 --> 00:31:15.152 align:middle line:90%
the game to.

00:31:15.152 --> 00:31:16.397 align:middle line:84%
Really, truly, I've
been thinking

00:31:16.397 --> 00:31:17.144 align:middle line:90%
about this one myself.

00:31:17.144 --> 00:31:22.124 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Usability testing,
by the way, is one of those

00:31:22.124 --> 00:31:24.655 align:middle line:84%
things that even if you only
have the chance to do a pop-up

00:31:24.655 --> 00:31:29.170 align:middle line:84%
partial test like, all I have
is this one card, the game's

00:31:29.170 --> 00:31:34.130 align:middle line:84%
not complete, but grab someone
walking down the corridor on

00:31:34.130 --> 00:31:37.939 align:middle line:84%
the way to the washroom and say,
come in here and take a

00:31:37.939 --> 00:31:38.980 align:middle line:84%
look at this thing
I'm working on.

00:31:38.980 --> 00:31:39.950 align:middle line:84%
Even that is better
information.

00:31:39.950 --> 00:31:42.990 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Hey kid,
come in here.

00:31:42.990 --> 00:31:46.120 align:middle line:84%
Come in here, I have something
to show you?

00:31:46.120 --> 00:31:46.590 align:middle line:90%
I have a surprise for you.

00:31:46.590 --> 00:31:47.530 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:47.530 --> 00:31:51.220 align:middle line:84%
SARA VERRILLI: Actually, you
can get going testing for

00:31:51.220 --> 00:31:52.090 align:middle line:90%
[? serious. ?]

00:31:52.090 --> 00:31:54.175 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Free candy.

00:31:54.175 --> 00:31:56.676 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: [? Mona ?] actually
calls it employee kidnapping.

00:31:56.676 --> 00:31:59.870 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:59.870 --> 00:32:07.400 align:middle line:84%
Of course, he's the creative
director, so he can do that.

00:32:07.400 --> 00:32:10.786 align:middle line:84%
SARA VERRILLI: It works best
if you're accosting people

00:32:10.786 --> 00:32:13.370 align:middle line:90%
whom you have power over.

00:32:13.370 --> 00:32:14.610 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: The audio thing, by
the way, is one of those

00:32:14.610 --> 00:32:17.730 align:middle line:84%
things that could be really
useful if people are difficult

00:32:17.730 --> 00:32:18.580 align:middle line:90%
[? pundits ?].

00:32:18.580 --> 00:32:22.415 align:middle line:84%
And so it might be one of those
things that you want to

00:32:22.415 --> 00:32:25.880 align:middle line:84%
keep in reserve, it's like, OK,
there is a problem here

00:32:25.880 --> 00:32:28.005 align:middle line:84%
and the team goes, I don't think
there's a problem here.

00:32:28.005 --> 00:32:30.280 align:middle line:84%
I don't understand why anyone's
having problems here.

00:32:30.280 --> 00:32:35.522 align:middle line:84%
Then you quit, because it's
kind of a bludgeon

00:32:35.522 --> 00:32:37.560 align:middle line:90%
actually on the team.

00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:40.410 align:middle line:84%
On one hand, if you have a good
agent team, they're going

00:32:40.410 --> 00:32:43.400 align:middle line:84%
hear it and go, oh my god, we
have a problem and immediately

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:44.417 align:middle line:90%
try to fix it.

00:32:44.417 --> 00:32:45.770 align:middle line:90%
But it's awesome.

00:32:45.770 --> 00:32:50.868 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, it's like,
well, do you need to do a

00:32:50.868 --> 00:32:54.510 align:middle line:84%
recording for every single
offer that we have?

00:32:54.510 --> 00:32:55.730 align:middle line:84%
That's something to
be known about, so

00:32:55.730 --> 00:32:57.456 align:middle line:90%
keep that in reserve.

00:32:57.456 --> 00:33:01.210 align:middle line:90%


00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:03.010 align:middle line:90%
I think that's pretty much it.

00:33:03.010 --> 00:33:09.310 align:middle line:90%


00:33:09.310 --> 00:33:12.610 align:middle line:84%
I have an exercise, but I
think [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:33:12.610 --> 00:33:14.410 align:middle line:90%
so I can turn that off.

00:33:14.410 --> 00:33:18.010 align:middle line:90%


00:33:18.010 --> 00:33:19.810 align:middle line:84%
Do you have any questions
while I

00:33:19.810 --> 00:33:21.310 align:middle line:90%
graph the data segments?

00:33:21.310 --> 00:33:21.610 align:middle line:90%
No?

00:33:21.610 --> 00:33:22.860 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:33:22.860 --> 00:33:23.160 align:middle line:90%